[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Dynamic vs Explicit Mapping and Datatypes in Elasticsearch"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When indexing data into Elasticsearch, defining how fields are stored and indexed is crucial for performance and search accuracy. Elasticsearch achieves this through mapping, which can be handled dynamically or explicitly."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Dynamic Mapping?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, Elasticsearch utilizes dynamic mapping. When you index a document containing previously unseen fields, Elasticsearch automatically detects the data type and adds the fields to the index mapping. While this offers great flexibility for rapid prototyping, it can lead to suboptimal data types for production environments."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Automatically guesses types like long, float, boolean, date, and keyword/text."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strings are typically mapped as both 'text' (analyzed for full-text search) and a 'keyword' sub-field (for aggregations and sorting)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Risk of mapping explosion if users ingest unpredictable schemas."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Explicit Mapping?"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explicit mapping involves manually defining your index schema, including field names and their specific datatypes, before you start indexing data. This gives you full control over how your data is stored, indexed, and analyzed."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"PUT /products
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "product_id": { "type": "keyword" },
      "title": { "type": "text" },
      "price": { "type": "scaled_float", "scaling_factor": 100 },
      "created_at": { "type": "date" }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Elasticsearch Datatypes"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right datatype is essential for query performance and storage optimization."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Text: Used for unstructured full-text search. Values are analyzed and tokenized."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keyword: Used for structured content such as IDs, email addresses, status codes, and tags. Not analyzed, optimized for exact matches and aggregations."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Numeric: Includes integer, long, float, double, scaled_float, etc."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Date: Supports JSON strings, milliseconds since epoch, or formatted date strings."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Production"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use explicit mappings in production to prevent unexpected datatype conflicts."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Disable or strictly control dynamic mapping using 'dynamic: false' or 'dynamic: strict' to avoid mapping explosions."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage multi-fields (such as mapping a string as both text and keyword) only when necessary to save cluster memory."}]
